A sorting algorithm is **stable** if it preserves the relative order of equal elements. For example, if two elements $A$ and $B$ have the same value, and $A$ appears before $B$ in the original array, then $A$ will still appear before $B$ in the sorted array.
Merge Sort **is stable**. This is ensured during the merge step: when two elements from the left and right subarrays are equal, the element from the **left subarray** is always chosen first.
```
// Example merge logic for stability:
if (left[i] <= right[j]) { // crucial: use <= not just <
    merged_array[k++] = left[i++];
} else {
    merged_array[k++] = right[j++];
}
```
